{
  "version": "rubric-v4",
  "description": "rubric-v3's coders and validity floor with two mapping changes that make the 0-10 scale usable and comparable across sources. First, scale.standardise = quantile-per-source: a source's density is put on its franchise's pooled scale before it is scored, so a work's score no longer depends on which sources happened to resolve it (Wikipedia's terser, zero-heavier plot sections vs Memory Alpha's fuller summaries moved the combined figure by up to ~1.6 points). Second, scale.transform = log: ln(1+d)/ln(1+saturation) replaces d/saturation, because densities are heavy-tailed and a linear map left the top half of the axis empty (ST Sci median sat at 3.9 of 10, only 18% of seasons above 5). Saturation itself stays a global p95, so a franchise comparison remains honest — Doctor Who's Sci still reads below Star Trek's. Fi is combined by max (see axes.fi.combineNotes).",
  "measurement": {
    "minWords": 200,
    "notes": "Minimum source-text length for a measurement. Below it a source still counts as covering an episode (published in the text basis) but contributes no score. TVmaze summaries have a 33-word median and 92% of them contain no lexicon term at all."
  },
  "scale": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 10,
    "transform": "log",
    "standardise": "quantile-per-source",
    "mapping": "per sub-signal: standardise the density on the franchise's pooled scale (quantile-per-source), then frac = min(1, ln(1 + d) / ln(1 + saturation)); axis score = max * sum(weight * frac) / sum(weight)",
    "notes": "per sub-signal: the density is first put on the franchise's common source scale, then mapped with frac = min(1, ln(1 + d) / ln(1 + saturation)); axis score = max * sum(weight * frac) / sum(weight). Saturation is the 95th percentile of that sub-signal's raw value over all valid measurements in the repository (`calibrate`), so a score of 10 is the top 5% of what has actually been measured rather than an extrapolation. The log transform exists because densities bunch: with a linear map the median of a sub-signal sits near one fifth of saturation and the top of the axis is empty, which misreads as absence rather than rarity. The quantile step exists because a density is measured per source and sources differ systematically; `calibrate` writes the per-source maps under calibration.standardisation. Raw densities are always published alongside scores, and each season also carries a percentile `rank`."
  },
